    {
      "s": "Bloomberg Tech",
      "t": "SpaceX Wins Government Approval to Launch Space Data Centers",
      "d": "The US Federal Communications Commission approved SpaceX’s request to launch as many as 1 million satellites for the creation of a network of data centers in orbit for complex artificial intelligence computing.",
      "u": "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-11-25/spacex-receives-government-approval-to-launch-space-data-centers",
      "m": "11. 25. PM 06:05",
      "ts": 1795629926000,
      "fullText": "Technology SpaceX Wins Government Approval to Launch Space Data Centers Facebook X LinkedIn Email Link Gift Expand SpaceX signage during the company's IPO at the Nasdaq MarketSite in New York on June 12. Photographer: Michael Nagle/Bloomberg Facebook X LinkedIn Email Link Gift Gift this article Contact us: Provide news feedback or report an error Confidential tip? Send a tip to our reporters Site feedback: Take our Survey New Window Facebook X LinkedIn Email Link Gift By Sana Pashankar November 25, 2026 at 6:05 PM UTC Updated on November 25, 2026 at 8:54 PM UTC Bookmark Save Takeaways by Bloomberg AI The US Federal Communications Commission approved SpaceX ’s request to launch as many as 1 million satellites for the creation of a network of data centers in orbit for complex artificial intelligence computing. The agency’s first-ever authorization of data centers in space makes way for a concept that was central to Elon Musk ’s pitch to investors ahead of SpaceX’s initial public offering in June. The company has cautioned that orbital AI data centers rely on unproven technologies and may never be commercially viable."
